u have to run through all of  those changes just  sitting in front of a computer  waiting for an experiment to run  and then you literally increment a  number by one or by some fraction  it gets  it gets to be a lot so with something  like dvc  we give it  all of the values for all of the hyper  parameters  and it sets up a queue of experiments  for us  so before you even get to the stage  where you run one this is what the table  looks like  and what this allows you to do  is let's say you're working on a team  with other machine learning engineers  and you're all running these same kind  of experiments  so before you run your queued up  experiments you might want to check with  them and be like hey  did y'all already try this set of hyper  parameter values  is this a direction that we should go in  and they can look and see this  combination right here before you run  anything and be like well  we haven't used these values yet so it'd  probably be worthwhile to  run those experiments  and what you would do when you're ready  to run them  is just you use this command this dvc  exp run run all and that runs every  experiment in your queue  no matter how many you have in that  queue it will execute all of them  so  that's something to keep in mind if  you're thinking about offloading some of  this training to  some kind of cloud environment  you don't have to sit there and just  watch them run you can come back and  look at them later and something else  that dvc does if you wanted to run these  experiments in parallel so that maybe  you know you speed up  your time for tuning or you have some  kind of resource constraint you can do  that  so dvc gives you a lot of flexibility  with running multiple experiments  but after you've run everything  then you probably want to look at the  results right so we show those results  in the table  and you can see now  we have all of these different  hyperparameter combinations and we have  all of these metrics associated with  them  so  each experiment as you can see over here  on the left has an id associated with it  these ids are how we are able to  throw in that reproducibility  if at any point you decide that one of  these experiments looks like it's  something you should dig into  you can just  take this  take the id of that experiment apply it  to your workspace and it will load in  all of your code hyperparams and data to  give you the exact results that you see  in this table  so  we're looking at this table after we've  run all of these experiments and you see  we have a pretty  pretty wide range of average precisions  all the way from 46  up to 50 percent  so maybe you decide that you need  another eye to look at things because  maybe something just feels a little  weird to you about this  so you decide you want to make up a plot  to show everybody on your team because  that's what we do right we take  experiments and we compare them  so you run that dvc plots diff command  with whatever experiment ids you want to  compare  and you get these plots  just like this  so  here you can see  you know the precision versus the recall  tpr versus the fpr  and all of these metrics are actually  coming straight from your training  script so it's not some magic that dvc  is doing  it's just referencing the json files  that you produce from your experiments  so that is all that's happening here  that's all that shows in the table as  well  so  you take these plots to your colleagues  and y'all sit around and talk about them  and decide you know what we need to run  more experiments  so you set up a queue of experiments  again and this time maybe you decide  well i'll have time to finish out the  rest of this great search  let's just  random  search some things  so we get these definitely random values  over here for our hyperparameters  again we queue them up just so that we  can check with our associates to see has  anybody seen  any values in this range of  hyperparameters  if not  of course we're just going to go ahead  and execute them so we'll run all of our  experiments and then we'll look at the  table again  and now you see  these values are looking really really  similar  so maybe there's something about this  90 to 125  range for the nest  maybe there's something to this  i don't know between 300 and 500 for the  men split  but  you're able to look at these metrics  right here you don't have to go into  some other tool this is just a table  that's in your terminal  like  that's it it's just a cli tool that you  use and it shows you all of this  information without you having to sign  up for anything you don't have to log in  anywhere  you don't have to make ap guide calls to  anything  it's just here  and you see your metrics really easily  you see your hyper params associated  with them really easily  and you decide you want to run more  experiments because those values are all  too close together so we set up our  queue again  and we have a different set of hyper  parameter values here  and we're just going to go ahead and do  our regular thing we'll run them and  we'll look at our table  and  you know it looks like something did a  little bit better  so maybe this is the direction we need  to go with our experiments but before we  do that  we'll probably consult with everybody on  the team one more time so we'll take  them this plot that has  an experiment from one of our previous  runs and it has an experiment from today  so we look at these and we definitely  see there's  a decent difference between them  so you'll talk with your team  figure out which direction you want to  go  and then just continue running these  experiments  so by the time you're finished you get  some table that looks a lot like this  and imagine back in the beginning before  we used a tool like this  trying to keep track of all of this so  so neatly  so  each of these experiments  has  data associated with it it has code  changes associated with them it has all  of these different hyper parameter  combinations associated with them  and  can you imagine having to keep track of  all of this just with  a spreadsheet and some zip files  that  i've i've had to do that before and it's  not fun and it's not easy and when you  do want to go back you have to hope  nobody else has messed up a row on the  spreadsheet  so having a table like this to look  through and having a tool that lets you  easily go back and forward through your  experiments as you try to figure out  which direction you actually want to go  before this model hits production  and not only before it hits production  but  what exactly do you deploy to pride now  you know the exact things that you have  to deploy to production to get the  results that you expect  so  hopefully this table like  took away some of that anxiety from  trying to do all of these experiments  and track them  just by yourself or even if you have a  team that you work with it's going to be  really really hard to keep track of all  of those experiments between everybody  and be able to share them compare them  this makes it easy to do  all of that  but i do want to just wrap up with a few  key takeaways to leave some question  some time for questions  but the first thing  adding reproducibility is so important  because you don't want to get to that  point where you do have this incredible  model and you showed everybody on your  team how well it performs  and then  everybody's in agreement that yes this  is the one that should definitely go to  pride and you have no idea what exactly  to deploy to pride so then you have to  start digging through all of these  changes going through git commits zip  files  and it gets messy really fast and it  takes a lot of time to just try to sort  through everything  but when you have reproducibility from  the beginning is just as simple as  referencing an id  and you get all of your changes back  and of course dvc helps you do this  because it's keeping track of everything  in the background  you don't have to worry about what your  hyper params are at the moment all you  have to do is run your experiments and  then you can go and look at the metrics  and how well they performed  and the last thing i really really want  to emphasize of course i was talking  about dvc today and of course i would  love it if you checked out bbc  but i totally understand that there are  a bunch of other tools out there that  are targeted at mlaps so  kind of my challenge to you for today is  just go check out some of those tools  maybe hopefully dvc is the one that you  really like but if it's not there's  stuff like mlflow intensive board and a  bunch of other products so just try them  out because these tools are here to make  your life easier you don't have to you  don't have to implement this thing you  don't have to write the code for it just  check out the tools see what works best  for your processes  and go with it because even though it  might be a pain you can always change  later  but  with that being said i want to wrap up  and leave some time for questions and  again you can ask me questions  now you can ask them on slack you can  ask me on twitter at flippedcoding  or if you really just want to dive into  dvc and you want some help getting  things set up or debugging  feel free to join our discord channel  a lot of people come in there just to  get help getting unstuck on their  projects and we have the time  so are there any questions  malaysia it's jennifer i'm i'm wondering  if you have uh  you know anything you'd like to see um  added to dvc that would be helpful  in  you know where do you see the expansion  there um  i think  honestly a lot of stuff that we have on  the road map are things that i've been  kind of asking for it's like  dvc is such a powerful tool that it'd be  nice to have  just commands that make it easier to get  started with experiments and that's on  the roadmap so  instead of just  like having to set up everything you can  just set up the part that does  experiments so i'm pretty excited about  that coming out soon that's great um  well that's something  to to uh  follow for audition maybe next year on  your wish list  um  thanks for thanks for joining us and uh  and your discussion and for being part  of pi data global 2021 and  thanks everyone here for  for joining
 

